  "text": "Years went by the way years do in a place like Skerrin, which is to say nobody noticed them until they had gone.\n\nThe harbour office got a new roof. The Kessocks' boat was sold and bought back and sold again. Maren's hair went the colour of the tide tables' oldest pages, and the eleventh step began to trouble her knee.\n\nErris Kessock grew up and did not leave, which surprised everyone but Maren. She took the tide tables first, because somebody had to, and then the log, because the hand that kept it had started to shake. She had a neat hand of her own. She wrote the way she had always asked the time: as if it mattered and as if it did not.\n\nThe night the key passed, there was no ceremony. Maren sat in the office with her coat on and her left glove in her pocket and did not go up. Erris came to the door at ten to twelve.\n\n\"What time is it?\" Maren said.\n\nErris looked at the sky and then at her wrist. \"Ten to.\"\n\n\"You'll be going up, then.\"\n\n\"I'll be going up.\"\n\nShe went. Maren listened to the door stick and give. She listened for the stair, which could not be heard from there, and heard it anyway. She did not look at the harbour. The water lay where water should lie.\n\nAt ten past, somewhere above her in the dark, a key turned once, and twice, and three times. The clock went on."
